                <pb facs="0249" n="147" sameAs="frus:frus1908/pg147"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 10388.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Consul General Denby
                        to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Consulate General</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1907-12-11"><hi rendition="#italic">December 11,
                        1907</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Denby reports that the Chinese local authorities have paid British subjects
                    50,000 Mexican and German subjects 30,000 Mexican losses on account of the
                    Shanghai riots in December, 1905, and that the American losses, about 11,000
                    Mexican, have not been presented. Mr. Denby calls attention to the fact that
                    department cabled the legation in 1906 to inform the Chinese Government that if
                    the British and German losses were paid the payment of the American losses would
                    be demanded and recommends that the legation be directed to act.)</p>
            </div>
